#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""script-census — real `sh` scripts under pelt and under dash, compared.

The differential corpus (tests/diff/) asks whether pelt answers a
clause as dash does. This asks the other question: do the scripts
people actually run — autoconf `configure`, the automake and libtool
helpers, Debian and Alpine package scripts, init scripts, git's test
library, small `#!/bin/sh` utilities — run to the same output, the same
exit status and the same files under pelt as under dash?

NO THIRD-PARTY SCRIPT IS IN THIS REPOSITORY. Each is named below by
package, version and the sha256 of the script file (the pins live in
tools/script-census.pins), and comes from one of two places:

  * a release tarball fetched by URL and REFUSED unless its sha256 is
    the one written here;
  * an image built here from a base image pinned by digest and a
    package snapshot pinned by date (the Containerfiles are below).

A script whose sha256 is not the pinned one is REFUSED, not run.

HOW A SCRIPT RUNS. Every run is its own throwaway container:
`podman run --rm --network none`, with nothing of the host mounted but
one scratch directory (`/s`), made for that run under --dir and removed
after it. The shell under test is `/s/bin/sh` in both runs — a copy of
the image's own dash, or a static pelt — so a script that prints or
records its shell's path prints the same. A small wrapper (ours, run by
the image's own /bin/sh) verifies the script's digest, does the row's
setup, runs `timeout T /s/bin/sh SCRIPT ARGS…` from an empty
environment, and lists what the run made, changed or removed (a listing
of directories, links and file digests taken before and after). Only the top-level script runs under the shell under test:
whatever it starts through `#!/bin/sh` is the image's.

THE VERDICT, per script, over all its runs:
  same      stdout byte for byte, exit status, stderr by presence, and
            the file listing all agree
  differs   one of them does not
  hang      pelt was killed by the timeout where dash finished
  unstable  dash disagrees with a second run of itself (the script
            prints a date, a pid, …): not counted either way

THE AIDED PASS (--aided). A script that stops at its first missing
piece says nothing about what lies behind it. With --aided the wrapper
first rewrites the script (the same rewritten copy for both shells, by
the sed program AIDED below) so that the gaps the first pass counted
most go around the shell: `test -x` and its kin run the image's
/usr/bin/test, `umask` is skipped, `trap … 13` names PIPE, and the
three `case` arms autoconf writes with a `/` inside a bracket
expression are spelled without one. The verdicts of that pass say what
is left once those are fixed; they are never reported as pelt's.

usage: tools/script-census --dir SCRATCH --pelt STATIC_PELT [options] [id …]
  --dir D        scratch and cache (downloads, extracted trees, logs);
                 nothing is written outside it
  --pelt P       a statically linked pelt (tools/build --static)
  --podman CMD   the podman command line (default `podman`; a private
                 store is `podman --root D/store --runroot D/run`)
  --build        build the three images first (needs the network)
  --pin          write tools/script-census.pins from what is fetched
                 instead of checking against it
  --list         print the set and stop
  --aided        the second pass (below)
  --tsv FILE     write the verdict table there (default D/scripts.tsv)
Exit 0 when every script ran (whatever the verdicts), 2 when the
harness could not run or a digest was refused. With --strict, exit 1
unless every verdict is `same`.
"""

